<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Coromandel</h1><p class="st-meta">1610 CE – 1825 CE · capital Pulicat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dutch East India Company's trading posts on India's south-east coast, centred on Pulicat, a hub of the Asian textile trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>) opened a factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Masulipatnam</a> in 1605 and in 1610 obtained permission from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara</a> ruler to settle at Pulicat, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-geldria">Fort Geldria</a> was built in 1613. Further factories followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Negapatnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadras">Sadras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bheemunipatnam">Bimlipatnam</a>, forming the governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textiles, printed and painted chintz above all, were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC's</a> key goods for buying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. The company bought cloth from weavers through Indian merchants and also traded slaves during famines. Pulicat housed the governor and a mint. In 1690 the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Negapatnam</a>, then back to Pulicat in 1781.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British competition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> eroded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade, and the British occupied the posts during the French Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> the remaining settlements were transferred to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1825.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-coromandel.jpg" alt="Dutch Coromandel" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1610 CE – 1825 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pulicat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Church, Hinduism, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Tamil, Telugu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dutch Coromandel Coast, Pulicat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=dutch-coromandel" aria-label="Dutch Coromandel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6882 1678 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch Coromandel lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7202 1934L5097 634M7202 1934L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5097 634h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7202 1934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7202 1934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pulicat. <a href="/atlas?year=1718&amp;focus=dutch-coromandel">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

